lklfuse: on Darwin, -o owner=caller gives each new node to whoever made it, in its directory's group

linuxfs mounts a volume whose owner wants ownership kept (fstab or vsdb `owners`, "Ignore ownership" off) with
default_permissions and no uid=/gid= mapping, so the Mac's kernel checks Linux's ids. Without owner=, every file,
directory, symlink and node a Mac user then made was LKL's (0:0): the user could not write or chmod it next, and a
4755 create left a setuid-root file on the disk for wherever it is next mounted with suid. HFS+ with ownership on
gives a new node to its creator, in the group of the directory it is made in (BSD's rule). With owner=caller, lklfuse
does the same: mknod, mkdir, symlink and create chown the new node to fuse_get_context()'s uid and the parent
directory's gid, which also keeps a Linux setgid directory's group. Only a node the call creates: create chowns by
its descriptor (0005's O_EXCL loop, unchanged); mknod, mkdir and symlink by name, through an O_PATH|O_NOFOLLOW
descriptor, and only when the name is LKL's (uid 0) and, unless a directory, its only link. lklfuse serves one
request at a time (0009), so nothing can put another file under the name in between; the check keeps that true if
it ever changes (owner=root and owner=UID:GID go through it too). With no FUSE request to take ids from, the node
keeps LKL's own. Linux's chown drops a setuid or setgid bit the create asked for (ATTR_KILL_SUID); the owner may set
it afterwards, on a file that is then the owner's. tests/lklfuse-ops-test.c checks each kind of node, a setgid and a
plain directory of another group, the 4755 case, an existing file, the by-name guard, and no caller.

